{"user": "I'm confused on how \"captures\" work - can you explain, in laymans terms as much as possible?", "user_ts": "2026-08-05T15:02:50.820056+00:00", "assistant": "Sure.\n\nIn Othello, you \u201ccapture\u201d by **trapping a straight line** of opponent discs between:\n\n- one of your discs already on the board, and\n- the new disc you place.\n\nThe line can be **horizontal, vertical, or diagonal**.\n\nExample: if a row looks like:\n\n**Black \u2013 White \u2013 White \u2013 empty**\n\nand Black plays on the empty square, it becomes:\n\n**Black \u2013 Black \u2013 Black \u2013 Black**\n\nbecause the two White discs were trapped between Black discs.\n\nImportant details:\n- You only flip discs in **straight lines**.\n- You can flip in **multiple directions at once** from one move.\n- A legal move must flip **at least one** opponent disc.\n- You do **not** capture discs just because they are nearby; they must be in an unbroken line ending with your disc.\n\nIn your shown board, the game is finished: **no empty squares remain**, so no more captures can happen.", "assistant_started_ts": "2026-08-05T15:02:51.708193+00:00", "assistant_ts": "2026-08-05T15:02:55.220206+00:00", "annotation_user": {"final": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "consensus": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "votes": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 3}, "confidence": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 1.0}, "per_model": {"gpt": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "sonnet": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "llama": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"]}, "n_valid": 3, "min_votes": 2, "no_majority": false, "needs_review": false, "arbiter_used": false}, "annotation_assistant": {"final": ["Direct Instruction"], "consensus": ["Direct Instruction"], "votes": {"Direct Instruction": 3}, "confidence": {"Direct Instruction": 1.0}, "per_model": {"llama": ["Direct Instruction"], "gpt": ["Direct Instruction"], "sonnet": ["Direct Instruction"]}, "n_valid": 3, "min_votes": 2, "no_majority": false, "needs_review": false, "arbiter_u